    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d748-a1">

                <pb facs="0710" n="612" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg612"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. White to the
                            Marquis of Salisbury.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1900-03-19"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">March 19,
                    1900</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>My <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lord</hi>: With reference to the detention of
                    American merchandise by the British authorities in South Africa, I have the
                    honor, under instructions from my Government, to inclose for your lordship’s
                    information copies of bills of lading representing shipments made by Messrs.
                    Arkell &amp; Douglas, a firm of New York merchants, by the steamships <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Maria, Beatrice</hi>, and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Mashona</hi>, destined for Delagoa Bay.</p>
                <p>It will be observed from the bills of lading above mentioned that the shipments
                    made by Messrs. Arkell &amp; Douglas, embrace all classes of general cargo, such
                    as kerosene oil, brooms, stoves, building materials, white meal, shovels,
                    turpentine, lard oil, plows, shellers, flour, etc.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry White.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
